# What a DECLARATION may say about a lent pointer type — the other half of the
# lend's rule (design.md §4.19, §4.20; panel 122 R3 and R4; the cut is panel
# 167's, 2026-09-20).
#
# WHY IT IS ITS OWN MODULE, AND THE SEAM IS THE ONE `check/lending.hero` NAMES
# IN ITS FIRST PARAGRAPH. That file exists because *"the question is about a
# POSITION, not about a type in hand"*, and these two clauses are not about a
# position at all: they read a function's declared RESULT and a record's
# declared FIELD, and they walk the declarations rather than the expressions.
# They were a guest there for the same reason the typing was before them, and
# the same seam took the typing out to `check/lend_types.hero` at panel 164.
#
# WHAT MADE THE CUT DUE. Defect 067's repair put the host at 331 lines in
# `suite_layout.hero`'s unit against §11's 300, and `layout` said so. Panel
# 167's resolution then widens both clauses from `cstr` to `ptr` — without them
# the field lease it adopts is not sound, which its completeness critic
# measured — so this is the file that is about to grow, and it grows where a
# reader looking for *what may a declaration say* will find it.
#
# THE TWO CLAUSES CLOSE A TWO-HOP ROUTE NO POSITION RULE CAN SEE.
# `return pass(c: s.cstr())` puts the lend in an argument position, which the
# position rule blesses, and returns a CALL RESULT, which no rule about lends
# can follow. Refusing the SIGNATURE closes the whole route, and it was measured
# free: every `-> cstr` in this tree is already inside an `extern` group.

use ast
use source
use check/state
use check/table
use lend_errors

# A function outside an `extern` group may not answer `cstr` (panel 122 R3).
#
# WHY THIS AND NOT A FLOW ANALYSIS. `return pass(c: s.cstr())` puts the lend in
# an argument position, which the rule above blesses, and returns a CALL
# RESULT, which no rule about lends can see. Both compiling seats at panel 122
# built it: silent at `check`, heap-use-after-free under the sanitizer. One
# signature rule closes the whole two-hop route, and it is measured FREE —
# every `-> cstr` in `selfhost/`, `examples/` and `tests/` is already inside an
# `extern` group.
#
# It has no `written_types` hole, and that was measured rather than assumed: a
# generic body cannot answer `cstr`, because `function escape<T>(...) -> T`
# returning a lend is `error[type_mismatch]` at the definition, before
# `ir/mono.hero` instantiates anything.
function no_cstr_out_of_heroes(@c: state.Checker, a: ast.Ast, s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl])
    for decl in decls
        match decl.kind
            .function_decl fd =>
                if fd.value.is_extern
                    continue
                declared = c.out.written_types[fd.value.result]

                if declared.is_err()
                    continue

                if table.get(c.out.types, declared.must()) == .cstr
                    state.push_diagnostic(
                        @c,
                        lend_errors.cstr_out_of_heroes(source.span_text(s, decl.name), a.types[fd.value.result].span)
                    )
            .record_decl | .constant_decl | .variant_decl | .test_decl => continue

# A `cstr` field on a record outside an `extern` group (panel 122 R4).
#
# `selfhost/emit/gate.hero` already refuses `cstr` as the element of an array, a
# map or a `T?`. A record FIELD was the hole, and it was the hole the emitter's
# own note sent people through: *"hold the pointer in a `record` and make an
# array of that instead"*. Measured at panel 122: silent at `check`, and the
# binary writes — heap-use-after-free on read. That note is rewritten in the
# same commit as this clause, because a diagnostic that prescribes a compile
# error is worse than one that says nothing.
#
# A GROUP'S record is untouched: there the fields are the header's, C owns the
# bytes, and § FFI says in as many words that a group field may be a `cstr`.
# Enumerated over the tree at the sitting: no `cstr` field outside a group
# exists, so the clause costs this corpus nothing.
function no_cstr_in_a_record(@c: state.Checker, a: ast.Ast, s: source.Source, decls: [ast.Decl])
    for decl in decls
        match decl.kind
            .record_decl rec =>
                if !rec.header.is_err()
                    continue

                for field in rec.fields
                    declared = c.out.written_types[field.ty]

                    if declared.is_err()
                        continue

                    if table.get(c.out.types, declared.must()) == .cstr
                        state.push_diagnostic(
                            @c,
                            lend_errors.cstr_in_a_record(holder: source.span_text(s, decl.name), field: source.span_text(s, field.name), span: a.types[field.ty].span)
                        )
            .function_decl | .constant_decl | .variant_decl | .test_decl => continue

# **WHETHER THIS RECORD'S OWN FIELDS ARE ALREADY REFUSED** — the question defect
# 067's clause asks before it fires (2026-09-20).
#
# One mistake gets one message. A record of this program holding a `cstr` is
# refused above, and every construction of it is then refused too: reporting
# both tells the reader to fix two things where there is one, and the second
# disappears the moment the first is fixed. `emit/ffi.hero` settled the same
# shape at defect 062 in the same direction — *"the type error above is the true
# one, and the count this line would add is unreliable the moment one type is
# wrong, so it waits for the author to fix the type and run again."*
#
# It does NOT reach a record whose fields are legal: `record Box { p: ptr }`
# checks clean today, so a lend parked in one is refused by the construction
# clause alone and rightly.
function holds_a_refused_field(c: state.Checker, decls: [ast.Decl], decl: i64) -> bool
    match decls[decl].kind
        .record_decl rec =>
            if !rec.header.is_err()
                return false

            for field in rec.fields
                declared = c.out.written_types[field.ty]

                if declared.is_err()
                    continue

                if table.get(c.out.types, declared.must()) == .cstr
                    return true

            return false
        .function_decl | .constant_decl | .variant_decl | .test_decl => return false

    return false
